# Installation

Rocci is currently built from source. You need a Rust toolchain, the Roc
compiler, and the native webview dependencies for your platform.

## Prerequisites

| Requirement | Why it is needed |
| --- | --- |
| Rust and Cargo | Build the Rocci workspace and command-line tools |
| Roc | Compile generated Roc programs and authored apps |
| Git | Clone the repository |
| Native webview libraries | Open the embedded Tao/Wry preview window |

> The project currently follows a Roc nightly toolchain. A mismatched compiler
> can reject syntax that is valid for the revision used by the repository.

## Build from source

```sh
git clone https://github.com/koliyo/rocci.git
cd rocci
cargo build --workspace
```

Confirm that the configuration parser and template compiler run:

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- validate
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- build examples/styling/Styling.rocci
```

## Platform notes

### macOS

Tao and Wry use the system WebKit view. The repository can also assemble an
ad-hoc-signed `.app` bundle for local development.

### Linux

Install the WebKitGTK development packages required by Wry. Package names vary
by distribution. Both X11 and Wayland behavior still need broader CI coverage.

### Windows

Wry uses the operating-system webview. Windows packaging is not implemented
yet, but development runs use the same local HTTP and window model.

## Verify the setup

Run the starter counter:

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- run examples/counter/Counter.rocci
```

The command prints a local URL and opens a window. To use only your browser,
add `--no-window`; the default browser-only port is `8000`.

Next, follow the [quickstart](/docs/getting-started/quickstart/).
